o in our do data set and  we now have for every one of those  programs all the engagement features and  if the household did not watch a program  ever they were filled in with zero which  made numerical sense in this context so  I'll be referring in a bit to what's  called a group and what I mean by that  is you can see that program a has X  number of features associated with it  and program B has X number of features  those features when you look at sort of  a data frame or a data table you don't  necessarily get the fact that those  columns those columns those X columns  are related to each other right that's  somewhat lost but the idea that we want  to take into account is that they are  actually a group of features for a  program so some modeling considers  considerations that we did we first  thought about can we cluster the data to  identify popular programs and that  seemed at first like a viable solution  but we quickly realized that we'd just  be picking up popular programs which was  an idea we wanted to identify programs  that were differentiating our relevant  audience from the rest of the DTV  universe so we knew we wanted to do  classification at that point  additionally there's kind of this  optimization aspect as I mentioned we  want to optimize reach among the  relevant  audience minimize to some extent the  overlap between the programs are  recommending we don't want the same  people seeing a billion ads for this  that sort of thing as well as the cost  of the final recommendation so obviously  buying an ad spot cost money depending  on the program and its popularity those  sorts of things  so typically modeling approaches for Big  Data takes on one of three options  somewhat so you can take a sample and a  statisticians that's always kind of our  first our first move you can move into a  parallel implementation which at AT&amp;T  Labs is is very common or in this case  so we ended up doing a split and conquer  and I'll talk a little bit about why 