nd speed and in our case it  handles sparsity very well so as i  mentioned that why the matrix you can  imagine that now on every household  watches every of each of the 75,000  programs so it did that well there was a  stable out of the box of limitation  there's a package in R that was great  interpretation so you have from tree  based methods very often kind of a  variable important score but again this  was not at the program level it was at  the program feature level so we did have  to roll that up to a program level and  there's no directionality to it so it's  not quite as intuitive but still helpful  information on the last and it did much  better so where have we been using this  I'll go first through performance  measures that you can see sort of power  two final ones performed here so miss  classification error exactly what it  sounds like on our test data we looked  at sensitivity and specificity and one  of the key challenges here is that high  specificity was easier to achieve at the  cost of sensitivity because of the fact  that the data the distribution of  household is very zero inflated meaning  if you think about all of the probes in  our universe here it may be the case at  a very small portion are within the  advertisers relevant audience so sort of  a an intuitive understanding of this say  that you have a hundred households two  of which are in the relevant audience if  you do a naive approach and you label  them all none relevant your true  negative rates gonna be a hundred  percent because you've got all 98 of  those right but your true negative rates  true positive rate is going to be zero  we also looked at top twenty percent  lift which is a pretty common business  metric all this does is we take our  predictions on the test data and we  order the probabilities of descending  order we cut off look at the top twenty  percent what percent of true positive is  do we capture there  so if we capture 80% of true positives  in the top 20% of predictions  that's a lift of four 80/20 and that's  really good so use cases we've done this  you would imagine that viewing habits on  television translates pretty nicely to  other TV shows that you watch and movies  that you may want to see in theaters so  those have been our two use cases so  we've been looking at what are called  tuning campaigns which is when you see  an ad that's like tune in to the show  next week at 9 and also theatrical  release so when a movie is coming out in  theaters so we had done for proofs of  concept and one of them was for a  superhero movie that came out and you'll  see here the results from group regular  lives regression extra boost in both  cases you see in the results in the top  10 programs some superhero movies so  that's kind of that's nice that's  intuitive --xg booths you see a few more  than the group lasso and I'll just  mention this on that index there it's  great if you can say reach 70 percent of  the target with a program but if you're  also reaching 70 percent of the DirecTV  universe of that program you haven't  actually done an enrichment of the  relevant audience there so it's not  super so what you really want to see is  a program that reaches 80 percent of the  relevant audience for example and maybe  20% of all D TV households so it's that  proportion there just that that easy  division that's giving us the index so  anywhere you see that being over one  that represents an enrichment of the  relevant audience from that program so  I'll just point out quickly you see some  programs in the group lasso where  they're sporting events and the index is  only slightly above one what tended to  happen quite a bit was that it picked  out really popular programs so sporting  events things that a lot of people were  tuning into whereas XG booths did a  really good job at finding programs that  had really good reach as well as a  pretty high index and we had some other  metrics that we tagged on for business  purposes to explain these results in an  intuitive manner performance metrics  here I'll just show you I'll point out  the lift for XG boost was 4.5 so it was  pretty good and miss classification  error was only three percent versus  twenty percent for group lasso  so I mentioned the computational piece  that this was sort of this quote-unquote  big data problem and this is the stuff  that I find really fun so how do you  code this up can we break some stuff and  then try again essentially and that's  what we did  so the typical our workflow we realized  very quickly would not scale we sort of  knew that going in so whether if you did  a tidy verse or in my case some big data  table user for your data processing and  then you know shiny ggplot2 Trello scope  all really nice for visualization and  then your modeling you have some really  good we we knew we wanted a tree base  method to start so that's why those were  there  we know that didn't work and so at that  point you sort of have to think about  well how do you choose a tool of all of  the ones you know are available to you  and so here are some of the things that  Rick and I thought quite a bit about so  what does our team already use and and  who do we have working with what tool  and and on what sorts of things we  really wanted to learn something new so  we had a reason to do it and then it's  really about how much time you have to  do it  existing data architecture and back-end  support is huge obviously you can only  use what's available to you and data  handling computational performance also  came into play quite a bit here the big  thing though as I mentioned doing  something seeing if it worked and if it  didn't trying something else so in the  big landscape of tools this always sort  of makes me chuckle it's just so big  this is where I think the meetups have  been hugely helpful and that you sort of  know what state of the art right now in  the data science community and among our  users as I mentioned we all work in our  and so that's sort of what we want to be  doing so we sort of found some things  that were familiar to us that our group  is using luckily we have some fantastic  computer scientists within labs who were  incredibly helpful but you see here so  we already were using Hadoop for other  things h2o and spark you know we're all  viable options here so we ended up doing  we work within an HDFS environment so  I'll just briefly explain sort of our  setup and as I mentioned of course  anything you can do is conditional on  this but so to start we have a Hadoop  framework that's where a data stored it  also you know we have access to  MapReduce and those sorts of things  yarn is the job scheduler and handles  how resources are allocated so that's a  shared framework across all the  researchers at Labs meaning that no one  person can take over the entire cluster  which was a little bit of a bummer for  us because we wanted the whole cluster  but yeah if you don't you can so then on  top of that I won't go too into detail  on all these but here are some of the  the tools that we considered so Hadoop  MapReduce you know I think some people  think it's becoming obsolete but  ultimately it still does stuff really  pretty well so it's a batch processing  tool it doesn't read anything in memory  so there is a time component to the  reading and the writing to disk and if  you're gonna be building a model it  needs to be paralyzed aband used to be  able to run in a distributed way spark  and h2o let me briefly say hive is sort  of like a sequel background to Hadoop so  it sits on top of it and you can do  interactive queries at scale that was  really helpful for some initial EBA when  we didn't know what we were working with  spark and h2o we also considered again  distributed frameworks here they handle  data a little bit differently so that  spark in particular reads data into  memory which is great and we were able  to do that but then you also need the  memory for computational overhead and  that's where we kind of ran into the  problem of we could only have so much  the cluster so of course we're here to  talk about our and luckily all of these  have interfaces in are so you can use  are on the front end and interact with  these things on the back end we ended up  using Simon Urbanek he's a colleague of  ours and he wrote HMR  which is to do MapReduce for are  essentially and that was really helpful  for a lot of our data processing and  wide casting sparkley are and are  sparkling in h2o are also really great  options for doing this for building  models through our on bigger data so the  next challenge we are now moving into  aid of the US and so I'm excited to hear  some talks later today and tomorrow  about moving a pipeline into production  because that's where we're headed  and in the meantime just huge thanks to  Rick who is as I mentioned been my  colleague and all this as well as our  other collaborators thank you  [Applause]

