ork in our  and so that's sort of what we want to be  doing so we sort of found some things  that were familiar to us that our group  is using luckily we have some fantastic  computer scientists within labs who were  incredibly helpful but you see here so  we already were using Hadoop for other  things h2o and spark you know we're all  viable options here so we ended up doing  we work within an HDFS environment so  I'll just briefly explain sort of our  setup and as I mentioned of course  anything you can do is conditional on  this but so to start we have a Hadoop  framework that's where a data stored it  also you know we have access to  MapReduce and those sorts of things  yarn is the job scheduler and handles  how resources are allocated so that's a  shared framework across all the  researchers at Labs meaning that no one  person can take over the entire cluster  which was a little bit of a bummer for  us because we wanted the whole cluster  but yeah if you don't you can so then on  top of that I won't go too into detail  on all these but here are some of the  the tools that we considered so Hadoop  MapReduce you know I think some people  think it's becoming obsolete but  ultimately it still does stuff really  pretty well so it's a batch processing  tool it doesn't read anything in memory  so there is a time component to the  reading and the writing to disk and if  you're gonna be building a model it  needs to be paralyzed aband used to be  able to run in a distributed way spark  and h2o let me briefly say hive is sort  of like a sequel background to Hadoop so  it sits on top of it and you can do  interactive queries at scale that was  really helpful for some initial EBA when  we didn't know what we were working with  spark and h2o we also considered again  distributed frameworks here they handle  data a little bit differently so that  spark in particular reads data into  memory which is great and we were able  to do that but then you also need the  memory for computational overhead and  that's where we kind of ran into the  problem of we could only have so much  the cluster so of course we're here to  talk about our and luckily all of these  have interfaces in are so you can use  are on the front end and interact with  these things on the back end we ended up  using Simon Urbanek he's a colleague of  ours and he wrote HMR  which is to do MapReduce for are  essentially and that was really helpful  for a lot of our data processing and  wide casting sparkley are and are  sparkling in h2o are also really great  options for doing this for building  models through our on bigger data so the  next challenge we are now moving into  aid of the US and so I'm excited to hear  some talks later today and tomorrow  about moving a pipeline into production  because that's where we're headed  and in the meantime just huge thanks to  Rick who is as I mentioned been my  colleague and all this as well as our  other collaborators thank you  [Applause]
 

